  "preamble": "Fronting and unrounding of the close back rounded vowel [u] to [i]. It is usually tied to a context. In early Latin, short medial u became i, especially next to l and before labials (lubet > libet, optumus > optimus). In Southern (Polish and Galician) Yiddish, the u vowel was fronted to i (Middle High German sunne 'sun' > Poylish zin, hunt 'dog' > hint). Many other apparent cases, such as Greek υ, went through an intermediate front rounded [y]. Those belong in the u > y and y > i cells.",
  "phoneticEffects": "Fronting, Unrounding",
  "languageExamples": [
    {
      "language": "Latin",
      "languageFamily": "Italic",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "Old Latin lubet 'it pleases' (< Proto-Italic *luβēt)",
          "to": "Classical Latin libet",
          "note": "Regular change of /u/ to /i/ next to /l/ and a labial consonant; compare clupeus > clipeus 'shield', lubīdō > libīdō 'desire'."
        },
        {
          "from": "Old Latin optumus 'best', maxumus 'greatest'",
          "to": "Classical Latin optimus, maximus",
          "note": "Medial short u before a labial was replaced by i in the classical spelling; the u forms survive as Old Latin and archaizing variants."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "language": "Southern Yiddish (Poylish, Galitsyaner)",
      "languageFamily": "West Germanic",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "Middle High German sunne 'sun'",
          "to": "Poylish זון [zɪn]",
          "note": "Northern (Litvish) and YIVO Yiddish keep the inherited vowel as [zʊn]; Southern Yiddish fronted short u to i."
        },
        {
          "from": "Middle High German hunt 'dog'",
          "to": "Poylish הונט [hɪnt]",
          "note": "Litvish and YIVO [hʊnt]."
        }
      ]
    },
